+# brcache in-flow restore ------------------------------------------------------
+# A package restored from the brcache binary cache has a `.brcached` marker
+# dropped into its build dir by $(BRCACHE_RESTORE_CMD), which runs as an
+# order-only prerequisite of the package's first stamp (see inner-generic-package
+# below). When the marker is present, each step recipe skips its expensive body
+# while `make` still touches the stamp — so the stamp chain is rebuilt in
+# dependency order for free and the cached files are used as-is. `brcache-cached`
+# is empty unless brcache is active AND this package's marker exists, so ordinary
+# (non-brcache) builds are byte-for-byte identical to upstream.
+ifneq ($(BRCACHE_RESTORE_CMD),)
+brcache-cached = $(wildcard $(@D)/.brcached)
+# No-op fallback: a hit package's restore target depends on every dependency's
+# restore target. Cache hits only depend on hits (key closure), which all get
+# an explicit restore rule below — but a dependency that never went through
+# inner-generic-package (special/virtual helpers) would have no target. This
+# pattern rule makes any such reference resolve as a cheap no-op; explicit
+# per-package rules take precedence over it.
+%-brcache-restore: ;
+else
+brcache-cached =
+endif
